   "file": "knowledge/01-robot-bodies.md",
   "intro": "<p>This area covers the physical shapes robots come in and the handful of spec-sheet numbers used to compare them: degrees of freedom, payload, reach, repeatability, and speed. Each body type (six-axis arm, SCARA, delta, gantry, cobot, mobile robot, humanoid) is a different trade between how far it reaches, how much it carries, how fast it moves, and how precisely it returns to a point.</p><p>On the cheese plate line the body is chosen station by station. The rate is gentle: 200 plates an hour gives each station 18 seconds per plate, so washdown rating, gripper fit, and how the ingredient arrives decide more than raw speed. A reasonable fit, offered as a judgment call rather than a rule: deltas over the apple and cheese belts, a SCARA at the cracker feeder, a washdown six-axis arm at the grape tote, a gantry or arm at the outfeed, mobile robots for moving totes and carts, and no humanoid on the line for now.</p>",
