   "file": "knowledge/02-inside-a-joint.md",
   "intro": "<p>Every rotating joint of an electric robot arm is a small machine of its own: a motor that makes torque, a gearbox that trades the motor's speed for force, an encoder that measures the angle, a brake that holds the arm when power is off, and an electronic drive that meters current into the motor many thousands of times a second. Some robots, mostly collaborative ones, add a torque sensor that lets the joint feel pushes from outside. This area opens one joint and names each part, where it sits, and the spec words people use when they buy or compare them. It ends with the two non-electric ways to move things, compressed air and pressurized oil.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, every pick station robot (grape, apple, cracker, cheese) carries one of these joint stacks per motorized axis, so a six-axis arm has six motors, six gearboxes, six brakes, and at least six encoders. Which body each station gets is a judgment call: this guide puts a washdown <a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/six-axis\" data-concept=\"six-axis\">six-axis arm</a> at the grape tote, a <a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/scara\" data-concept=\"scara\">SCARA</a> at the cracker feeder, and <a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/delta\" data-concept=\"delta\">deltas</a> at the apple and cheese belts, while this area pictures a six-axis arm wherever a joint example needs one. The simpler motions, such as separating trays at the denester or working the grippers that hold each ingredient, are mostly done with compressed air instead.</p>",
