   "file": "knowledge/03-end-effectors.md",
   "intro": "<p>A robot arm on its own can only carry its wrist to a point in space. The part that actually touches the product is the end effector bolted to that wrist. This area covers how that tool is described to the robot (the tool center point), the three main ways to hold things (vacuum, soft fingers, rigid fingers), the tool changer that lets one arm swap tools, and the force-torque sensor that gives the wrist a sense of touch.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, each of the four pick stations needs a different end effector, because each ingredient fails in its own way. A grape bunch rolls and sheds berries. Apple slices are wet. Cracker packs are floppy film. Cheese is fatty and dents. Everything that touches unwrapped food must be made of food-contact material and must survive washdown in a 36-40 F (2-4 C) room.</p>",
