   "file": "knowledge/04-sensors.md",
   "intro": "<p>A robot arm with no sensors replays the same motion forever, so everything it handles must arrive in exactly the same place every time. Sensors are how a robot cell notices what actually arrived: cameras that see where a cheese wedge landed, depth cameras that measure how tall a grape bunch is, lidar and safety scanners that notice a person walking up, photo-eyes that report &quot;a tray is here&quot;, and IMUs that sense tilt and motion. This area covers the hardware specs that decide whether a sensor can do its job, and then calibration: the measurements that turn a camera's pixels into millimeters in the robot's own coordinates.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, photo-eyes tell the PLC (the line's industrial controller) when a tray reaches each station; cameras above the grape, apple, and cheese pick stations find each ingredient; a color camera at inspection checks that every item is on the plate before lidding; and a safety scanner watches the spot where an operator walks up to pull rejects. None of the vision works until each camera is calibrated and its position relative to its robot is known to within a millimeter or two.</p>",
