   "file": "knowledge/07-classical-software.md",
   "intro": "<p>&quot;Classical&quot; robot software is the hand-written, mostly deterministic (the same inputs always give the same outputs) stack that turns a request like &quot;put this cheese wedge in that compartment&quot; into motor currents. It has four layers. The math layer is coordinate frames and kinematics. The search layer is motion planning and collision checking. The timing layer is trajectory generation, control loops and conveyor tracking. The logic and tooling layer is state machines, perception, grasp planning, ROS 2, MoveIt, OpenCV, vendor robot languages and PLC programming. Every step is engineered and inspectable: when a pick fails you can usually point to the step that went wrong (bad calibration, no IK solution, a planner timeout, an overshooting joint). <a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/learned-policy\" data-concept=\"learned-policy\">Learned policies</a> and the rest of area 8 cover learned models that replace parts of this pipeline.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, every pick station runs much of this stack every few seconds. The camera finds an apple slice on its infeed belt (perception) and its position is converted from camera coordinates to robot coordinates (frames). The software chooses how to hold the slice (grasp planning), solves for joint angles (inverse kinematics), gets a path that misses the tray walls, either taught or planned (motion planning and collision checking), times that path (trajectory generation) while keeping up with the slice as the belt carries it (conveyor tracking), and each joint's drive follows the result (control loops). A state machine sequences the steps, and the PLC coordinates the stations. This file covers the math-and-motion half first, then the logic, perception and tooling half.</p>",
