   "file": "knowledge/08-robot-learning.md",
   "intro": "<p>Robot learning covers the methods that produce robot behavior from data instead of from hand-written rules. These include copying human demonstrations (imitation learning), trial and error against a score (reinforcement learning), practicing in simulation and moving the skill to hardware (sim-to-real), and large pretrained models that take camera images plus a text instruction and output arm motions (vision-language-action models). The area also covers the less glamorous work that decides whether any of it succeeds: collecting datasets, measuring success honestly, and running the network fast enough on the robot.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, robot learning is most useful where the objects change from plate to plate. That means the grape pick (tangled, soft bunches) and the apple pick (wet, irregular slices). The tray denester, cracker pick, and lidding handle rigid, repeatable items, where classical programming is usually cheaper and easier to validate. As of late 2026, most of the learned systems named here are research systems or pilots. Each product entry gives the deployment status where I could find it.</p>",
