   "file": "knowledge/09-teleoperation.md",
   "intro": "<p>Teleoperation is a person controlling or guiding a robot from somewhere else. This area covers the two main styles (giving the robot a hint versus driving it directly), the controls an operator holds, and the plumbing in between: how a camera frame becomes a compressed video stream, how WebRTC carries it through firewalls, why live video and commands ride on UDP rather than TCP, what jitter and packet loss do, and how watchdogs make the robot stop safely when the link dies. The thread running through all of it is the latency budget: an itemized account of every millisecond between something happening at the robot and the robot responding to the operator.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, teleoperation shows up when a pick fails. The grape pick station is the usual culprit: a tangled bunch hides its stem, the vision system loses confidence, and the station asks a remote operator for help. Most of the time the operator clicks one point on a still image and the robot finishes the job itself. Occasionally the operator takes over the arm directly through a live video feed, and then every stage of the latency budget matters.</p>",
