   "intro": "<p>This area covers the software around the robots rather than inside them: the services that watch a fleet, record what each robot sensed and did, show that data to engineers, push out new software and new models, and turn recordings into training data. If you have run a web platform, most of the shapes will look familiar. Three things change. The data is much bigger (cameras and depth sensors produce far more bytes than request logs). The network is worse (a robot on a factory floor may have a weak or intermittent link). And a bad deploy can move a metal arm, not just return an error page.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, this is the layer that tells the plant manager the cheese station has slowed down, keeps the last few minutes of camera and arm data when a grape bunch goes missing, lets an engineer replay that moment at a desk, tries a new grape-picking model alongside the old one before letting it drive the arm, and ships operators' corrections back to the training team. Ride 4 follows that loop: recording, cloud, training data, new model, fleet.</p>",
