   "intro": "<p>A robot cell does not arrive in a box and start working. Between &quot;we should automate this&quot; and a cell running at rate, a plant goes through a fixed sequence: scope the business case, design the cell on a computer, buy it, test it at the builder's shop and again at the plant, start it up, and then keep it running for years. Each phase has its own documents, tests, and people, and each one is a place where a project can stall even when the robot itself is fine.</p><p>This area walks the cheese plate line through every phase, from the first question (is a 200-plates-an-hour robotic line worth the money?) to the last (who fixes the grape gripper in the middle of the night shift?). It ends with the alternative to buying at all: paying a provider by the hour or by the month to own and run the robots for you.</p>",
