   "intro": "<p>This area is a map of the companies that make up the robotics industry and of who pays whom. A working robot line is never the product of one company. Parts makers build gears, motors, grippers, and cameras. Robot makers turn some of those parts into arms with controllers. Integrators buy arms and everything around them and build one line for one plant. The plant, the end user, pays for all of it. Newer kinds of companies cut across these layers: application companies that sell a finished robot station for one job, model companies that train the learned &quot;brains&quot;, and tooling companies that sell software to everyone else.</p><p>On the cheese plate line, this is the cast list. An airline caterer is the end user. A food-specialized integrator, or an application company that already sells food-assembly robots, builds the stations. A robot maker supplies the four pick arms. Component makers supply the gears inside those arms and the vacuum cups and soft fingers on the end of them. Tooling companies supply the simulation and data software used to design and debug the line. Each company-type concept below lists what that kind of company sells, who it buys from and sells to, the jobs inside it, and real examples checked as of November 2026.</p>",
