   "file": "knowledge/01-robot-bodies.md",
   "plain": "<p>An Etch A Sketch has two knobs, so you can steer its pen in two independent ways: left-right and up-down. A robot's degrees of freedom are its independent &quot;knobs&quot;, the number of separate motions you must set to describe exactly where every part of it is. To put a tool anywhere in a room and also point it any way you like, you need six: three for position and three for which way it faces. Robots with fewer can still do useful work, as long as the job never needs the missing motions.</p>",
   "analogyLimit": "<p>Each Etch A Sketch knob maps straight to one direction, but most robot joints rotate, so turning one joint swings the tool along a curve and the joints' effects mix together. Counting motors or joints also does not always give the DoF: a delta robot has many unpowered hinges but only three or four independent motions.</p>",
   "precise": "<p>A robot's degrees of freedom (DoF) are the smallest number of real-valued coordinates needed to describe its configuration (the position of every point on the robot) <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>. A rigid object floating in space has 6 DoF, three for position and three for orientation (which way it points); a rigid object sliding on a table has 3 <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>. The two common robot joints each add 1 DoF: a revolute joint (a hinge that rotates about one axis) and a prismatic joint (a slide that moves along one axis) <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>. The set of all possible configurations is called the configuration space, a space with one dimension per DoF <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</p><p>In an open chain (links connected one after another with no loops, like a typical arm), each motorized joint adds one DoF, so a 6-joint arm has 6 DoF. In a closed chain (links that form loops, like the three arms of a delta robot that all meet at one plate), you count with Grübler's formula, which subtracts the constraints each loop adds; it assumes those constraints are independent, so it can give the wrong answer for mechanisms where they are not <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>. Spec sheets usually say &quot;axes&quot; instead of DoF and count the motorized ones.</p><p>The task decides how many DoF are needed. Placing a part from above onto a flat belt needs only x, y, z, and a turn about vertical: 4 DoF, which is why SCARAs and many delta robots are 4-axis machines <sup class=\"cite\">[3, 5]</sup>. Reaching around obstacles at any angle needs 6. A seventh makes an arm redundant (more joints than the task needs), so it can move its elbow out of the way while the tool stays still <sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>. Without enough DoF, some tool poses are simply impossible, however good the software is.</p>",
   "cheese": "<p>The apple station only lifts a slice off the infeed belt, carries it across, turns it to match the tray compartment, and sets it down: x, y, z plus one rotation, which a 4-axis delta or SCARA provides. The grape station is different, because bunches lie in a tote at random angles and the gripper may need to tilt to slide under a stem, which takes the wrist motions of a 6-axis arm. This split is a judgment call, not a sourced rule.</p>",
   "elsewhere": "<p>In a car plant, FANUC's catalog shows the DoF count being picked per job: its R-1000iA family, whose /100F model FANUC pitches for high-speed spot welding, comes as 6-axis models, a 5-axis /80H variant, and a 7-axis /120F-7B variant, and its small M-1iA delta comes in 3-, 4-, and 6-axis versions <sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>. At the far end, Boston Dynamics lists 56 DoF for its electric Atlas humanoid <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>, because legs, torso, arms, and hands each need their own motions.</p>",
   "why": "<p>DoF is the first filter when matching a robot to a task: count the independent motions the tool must make, and any body with fewer cannot do the job. It also sets the size of the math problem the controller must solve to turn a desired tool pose into joint angles.</p>",
