   "products": "<ul><li><strong>Basler ace 2 (Basler AG)</strong> — compact industrial cameras in USB 3.0, GigE, and sealed IP67 GigE versions; the a2A1920-160ucBAS is a 2.3 MP global shutter color model <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></li><li><strong>Sony IMX392</strong> — the 2.3 MP global shutter sensor inside those two ace 2 models <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></li><li><strong>C-mount lenses</strong> — the standard screw-in lens mount used by the ace 2 and many other industrial cameras <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></li><li><strong>Keyence lighting selection guide</strong> — a vendor reference for choosing ring, bar, dome, backlight, coaxial, and low-angle lights <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></li></ul>",
   "misconception": {
    "myth": "<p>A higher-resolution camera is the fix for an unreliable vision station.</p>",
    "reality": "<p>Most failures come from glare, shadows, blur, or weak contrast, which more pixels do not fix; Keyence ranks lighting among the most important factors in reliable inspection <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>. More pixels also mean more data per picture, and a cable carries a fixed number of bits per second: this 2.3 MP camera already tops out at 51 fps on GigE <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>, so a camera with twice the pixels on the same cable would manage only about half that, and processing slows too.</p>"
   },
   "keyNumbers": "<ul><li>Frame rate: 51-164 fps for the same 2.3 MP sensor, depending on the interface (GigE or USB 3.0) <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></li><li>Exposure time: 19 µs to 10 s on the a2A1920-160ucBAS, or 1-14 µs in its ultra-short mode <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></li><li>Pixel size: 3.45 µm on the IMX392 <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></li><li>Data rate check: 1920 x 1200 pixels x 8 bits x 164 fps ≈ 3 Gbit/s, under USB 3.0's 5 Gbit/s nominal maximum <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup> but about three times what gigabit Ethernet carries, which is why the GigE version stops near 51 fps <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></li><li>Motion blur in pixels = object speed x exposure time / millimeters per pixel</li></ul>",
   "specs": null,
   "code": null,
