   "products": "<ul><li><strong>Dex-Net and GQ-CNN</strong> — Berkeley AUTOLAB grasp datasets and grasp quality networks for parallel jaws and suction <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[7]</sup></li><li><strong>GPD (Grasp Pose Detection)</strong> — open-source antipodal grasp detector for point clouds <sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup></li><li><strong>Contact-GraspNet</strong> — 6-DoF parallel-jaw grasp network for cluttered scenes <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></li><li><strong>AnyGrasp SDK</strong> — grasp perception SDK from the AnyGrasp authors <sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup></li><li><strong>Ambi Robotics</strong> — sells AI-powered parcel sorting (AmbiSort) and stacking (AmbiStack) systems as of 2026 <sup class=\"cite\">[9]</sup></li></ul>",
   "misconception": {
    "myth": "<p>The highest-scoring grasp is the one to execute.</p>",
    "reality": "<p>A quality score rates the contact, not the whole pick. A top-scoring grasp may be unreachable, collide on approach, or leave the item turned the wrong way for its tray slot, so planners rank candidates and walk down the list until one passes reachability, collision, and placement checks. A failed attempt can then fall back to the next candidate without taking a new image.</p>"
   },
   "keyNumbers": "<ul><li>Dex-Net 2.0: 0.8 s planning time, 93% success on eight known objects with adversarial shapes <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup></li><li>Dex-Net 3.0 suction: 98%, 82%, and 58% success on basic, typical, and adversarial novel objects <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup></li><li>Dex-Net 4.0: over 95% reliability and over 300 mean picks per hour, bins of up to 25 novel objects <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup></li><li>AnyGrasp: 93.3% success and over 900 mean picks per hour on 300+ unseen objects <sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup></li><li>Contact-GraspNet: over 90% success on unseen objects in structured clutter <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup></li><li>These depend on object set, gripper, and test protocol, so they are not directly comparable with each other</li></ul>",
   "specs": null,
   "code": {
    "lang": "python",
    "text": "import numpy as np\n\nCUP_R = 0.010                                    # 20 mm suction cup\n\ndef suction_candidates(points, normals, centroid, neighbors, n=200):\n    \"\"\"points, normals: Nx3 arrays for ONE segmented object, robot base frame, meters.\"\"\"\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n    scored = []\n    for i in rng.choice(len(points), size=min(n, len(points)), replace=False):\n        p, nrm = points[i], normals[i]\n        disk = points[np.linalg.norm(points - p, axis=1) < CUP_R]\n        if len(disk) < 30:\n            continue                             # not enough surface under the cup to seal\n        flat_rms = np.sqrt(np.mean(((disk - p) @ nrm) ** 2))   # how far points sit off the plane\n        seal = np.exp(-flat_rms / 0.0005)        # near 1 when flatter than about 0.5 mm\n        center = np.exp(-np.linalg.norm(p - centroid) / 0.015)  # prefer the balance point\n        upright = max(0.0, nrm[2])               # cup comes from above\n        clear = len(neighbors) == 0 or np.min(np.linalg.norm(neighbors - p, axis=1)) > 0.02\n        scored.append((seal * center * upright * clear, p, -nrm))  # approach along -normal\n    return sorted(scored, key=lambda c: -c[0])[:5]   # caller still checks IK and collisions\n\ndef antipodal_ok(p1, n1, p2, n2, mu=0.4):\n    \"\"\"Two finger contacts with outward surface normals n1, n2; mu = friction coefficient.\"\"\"\n    axis = (p2 - p1) / np.linalg.norm(p2 - p1)\n    half_angle = np.arctan(mu)                   # friction cone half-angle\n    a1 = np.arccos(np.clip(np.dot(-n1, axis), -1.0, 1.0))   # finger 1 pushes along +axis\n    a2 = np.arccos(np.clip(np.dot(-n2, -axis), -1.0, 1.0))  # finger 2 pushes along -axis\n    return a1 < half_angle and a2 < half_angle   # both squeezes stay inside their cones",
    "html": "<pre class=\"code\"><code data-lang=\"python\">import numpy as np\n\nCUP_R = 0.010                                    # 20 mm suction cup\n\ndef suction_candidates(points, normals, centroid, neighbors, n=200):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;points, normals: Nx3 arrays for ONE segmented object, robot base frame, meters.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(0)\n    scored = []\n    for i in rng.choice(len(points), size=min(n, len(points)), replace=False):\n        p, nrm = points[i], normals[i]\n        disk = points[np.linalg.norm(points - p, axis=1) &lt; CUP_R]\n        if len(disk) &lt; 30:\n            continue                             # not enough surface under the cup to seal\n        flat_rms = np.sqrt(np.mean(((disk - p) @ nrm) ** 2))   # how far points sit off the plane\n        seal = np.exp(-flat_rms / 0.0005)        # near 1 when flatter than about 0.5 mm\n        center = np.exp(-np.linalg.norm(p - centroid) / 0.015)  # prefer the balance point\n        upright = max(0.0, nrm[2])               # cup comes from above\n        clear = len(neighbors) == 0 or np.min(np.linalg.norm(neighbors - p, axis=1)) &gt; 0.02\n        scored.append((seal * center * upright * clear, p, -nrm))  # approach along -normal\n    return sorted(scored, key=lambda c: -c[0])[:5]   # caller still checks IK and collisions\n\ndef antipodal_ok(p1, n1, p2, n2, mu=0.4):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Two finger contacts with outward surface normals n1, n2; mu = friction coefficient.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    axis = (p2 - p1) / np.linalg.norm(p2 - p1)\n    half_angle = np.arctan(mu)                   # friction cone half-angle\n    a1 = np.arccos(np.clip(np.dot(-n1, axis), -1.0, 1.0))   # finger 1 pushes along +axis\n    a2 = np.arccos(np.clip(np.dot(-n2, -axis), -1.0, 1.0))  # finger 2 pushes along -axis\n    return a1 &lt; half_angle and a2 &lt; half_angle   # both squeezes stay inside their cones</code></pre>"
   },
