   "products": "<ul><li><strong>RViz2</strong> — ROS 2's plugin-based 3D viewer; heavy on graphics, so Stereolabs, for one, advises running it on a separate computer rather than the robot's embedded board <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</li><li><strong>Foxglove</strong> — commercial visualization and data platform; free tier, Pro from $20 a month as of May 2026 <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li><li><strong>Lichtblick</strong> — open-source viewer in the browser or as a desktop app <sup class=\"cite\">[3]</sup>.</li><li><strong>Rerun</strong> — open-source logging SDK and viewer with its own .rrd format <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>.</li><li><strong>PlotJuggler</strong> — open-source time-series plotting tool, named in 2022 as able to read MCAP <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>.</li><li><strong>LeRobot dataset viewer</strong> — opens a recorded training episode in Rerun, or serves it to Foxglove <sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li></ul>",
   "misconception": {
    "myth": "<p>Visualization is a nice extra for demos.</p>",
    "reality": "<p>For most robot bugs it is the main debugging tool, the way a debugger and distributed tracing are for services. If the viewer cannot show what the robot believed at the failing moment, the team is mostly guessing.</p>"
   },
   "keyNumbers": "<ul><li>Foxglove Studio's last open-source release: v1.87.0, February 2024 <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li><li>Foxglove free tier as of May 2026: 3 developer seats, 10 GB cloud storage, 5 connected devices <sup class=\"cite\">[2]</sup>.</li></ul>",
