    "companies": "<ul><li><strong>FANUC</strong> (Japan) — independent and listed on the Tokyo Stock Exchange; robots, CNC controls, and machine tools; more than 1 million robots installed <sup class=\"cite\">[10]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[13]</sup>.</li><li><strong>ABB Robotics</strong> (part of ABB, Switzerland) — still part of ABB as of November 2026. ABB agreed in October 2025 to sell it to SoftBank Group for an enterprise value of US$5.375 billion <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>. In November 2026 ABB said China's foreign-investment security review had prohibited the acquisition of the ABB Robotics business in China, and that ABB and SoftBank were still working on the transaction <sup class=\"cite\">[15]</sup>.</li><li><strong>KUKA</strong> (Germany) — owned by Midea Group: control from January 2017 (a 94.55% stake), remaining shares squeezed out in November 2022; KUKA also owns Swisslog and Visual Components <sup class=\"cite\">[6]</sup>.</li><li><strong>Universal Robots</strong> (Denmark) — owned by Teradyne since 2015 (US$285 million); grouped with MiR as Teradyne Robotics <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup><sup class=\"cite\">[8]</sup>.</li><li><strong>Boston Dynamics</strong> (US) — Hyundai Motor Group holds 80% and a SoftBank affiliate 20% <sup class=\"cite\">[18]</sup>; Rohit Prasad, formerly an Amazon AI executive, became CEO in October 2026 <sup class=\"cite\">[19]</sup>.</li><li><strong>Stäubli</strong> (Switzerland) — robotics is one of four divisions, alongside electrical connectors, fluid connectors, and textile machinery <sup class=\"cite\">[14]</sup>.</li></ul>"
