     "plain": "<p>The camera turns the light into a picture: a grid of tiny squares, each recording how bright one spot was. The grape station's camera also measures how far away each spot is. That is like a map that shows the height of every hill, not just its outline, so the software knows how tall each heap of grapes is.</p>",
     "precise": "<p>A camera's image sensor is a chip covered in a grid of light-measuring squares called pixels. Exposure time (how long the pixels collect light for each picture) sets blur: a moving item smears by its speed times the exposure, and a tote of bunches sits still. Most industrial cameras use triggered acquisition (each picture starts when a signal arrives on an input wire), so every picture belongs to a known moment.</p><p>A <a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/depth-camera\" data-concept=\"depth-camera\">depth camera</a> adds a distance to each pixel, giving a point cloud (a list of 3D points). Area 4's grape example uses a structured light camera (it projects known patterns and measures how surfaces bend them), such as the Zivid 2+ MR60. From 600 mm it sees about 570 x 460 mm with about 0.24 mm between points, enough to find a stem a few millimeters thick (illustrative design). The catch: the scene must hold still for each capture, which takes 25-1500 ms depending on settings. Stereo cameras, the other common kind, have a different weakness: their depth error grows with the square of distance, so mounting height matters. The Zivid lists IP65 (proof against low-pressure water jets), so it is covered during sanitation rather than sprayed (illustrative design choice).</p><p>Without depth, the robot must assume every bunch lies at one known height, which fails as soon as bunches heap up or tilt.</p>",
     "question": {
      "prompt": "<p>To see more of the tote, someone proposes mounting a stereo depth camera twice as high. Area 4's worked example puts its depth error at about 2 mm at 1 m. What should they expect at 2 m?</p>",
      "options": [
       {
        "text": "About 1 mm, since a wider view averages out the error",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Distance makes stereo depth worse, not better, which is why area 4's apple example moves its plate-mounted camera in close for the final look."
       },
       {
        "text": "About 2 mm, since datasheet accuracy holds at any distance",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Datasheet figures come from friendly test conditions, and stereo error changes a lot with distance."
       },
       {
        "text": "About 4 mm, since error doubles when distance doubles",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Doubling is the rule for a small angle error at a distance; stereo depth error grows faster than that."
       },
       {
        "text": "About 8 mm, since stereo error grows with the square of distance",
        "correct": true,
        "why": "Far objects shift fewer pixels between the two views, so the same fraction-of-a-pixel matching error costs four times the depth at twice the distance."
       }
      ]
     }
