     "plain": "<p>Software looks at the picture and draws an outline around one bunch it thinks it can pick. It works like someone sorting laundry from a heap: they do not study every thread, they decide &quot;that one, right there&quot; and reach. The software also says how sure it is, and if it is not sure enough, it asks for a new picture instead of guessing.</p>",
     "precise": "<p><a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/perception\" data-concept=\"perception\">Perception</a> (turning images into the few facts a pick needs) runs on the vision PC: an industrial PC with a GPU (a chip that does the same arithmetic on many numbers at once, which neural networks need) in the line's control cabinet.</p><p>Classical rules such as thresholding (marking each pixel as object or background by comparing it to a cutoff) work for uniform items like cheese wedges on a dark belt, but they break when objects vary. Bunches tangle and vary, so the grape station is the natural place for a trained instance-segmentation model (a network that outputs the exact pixels of each object, called a mask, plus a class and a score). A small current model, Ultralytics YOLO26n-seg, takes about 2 ms per image on an NVIDIA T4 GPU. Pipelines are assembled from libraries such as OpenCV, but the recognition comes from the model, not the library.</p><p>Each detection carries a confidence (a 0-1 score). It is not a measured probability, because modern networks tend to be overconfident, so the accept threshold is set by counting hits and misses on images from the real line. Below it, the cell's sequencing logic (a state machine or behavior tree that decides the next step) takes a new picture or skips. Without this stop, the robot does not know where the bunch actually is.</p>",
     "question": {
      "prompt": "<p>The grape model marks a bunch with confidence 0.95. What does that number tell you?</p>",
      "options": [
       {
        "text": "The pick will succeed 95% of the time, so about 1 pick in 20 will fail",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "It scores the detection, not the whole pick, and even as a detection score it is not a measured probability."
       },
       {
        "text": "A score: how often 0.95 is right must be measured on the line",
        "correct": true,
        "why": "The count has to come from images from the real line. Networks are often overconfident, so teams set thresholds from counted hits and misses, and recount after any change of lighting, camera, or product."
       },
       {
        "text": "95% of the pixels inside the outline belong to the bunch, not the background",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "The mask lists the pixels; the confidence is a separate score for the detection as a whole."
       },
       {
        "text": "Nothing useful, so every detection should be used whatever its score",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "A low-confidence detection is a normal failure that the sequencing logic routes to a new picture or a skip."
       }
      ]
     }
