     "mode": "classical",
     "replaces": [],
     "plain": "<p>Knowing where the bunch is does not yet say where to grab it or from which direction. The software picks a spot, often near the stem, and a direction for the fingers to come in, tilted if the bunch lies at an angle. It is like reaching into a fruit bowl: you choose your grip before your hand moves.</p>",
     "precise": "<p><a class=\"concept-link\" href=\"#concept/grasp-planning\" data-concept=\"grasp-planning\">Grasp planning</a> outputs a grasp pose (where the gripper's working point goes and how it is turned), an approach direction with a pre-grasp point a few centimeters back along it, and a grasp quality score (an estimate of the chance the grasp holds through the move). Bunches lie at random angles, so the pose is a full six numbers: three for position, three for tilt and turn.</p><p>This guide offers two designs, both judgment calls. Area 7 pictures fingers closing across the main stem, which needs vision to find a stem segment visible on both sides and clear of berries. Area 3 pictures three or four soft fingers closing around the bunch from above at low pressure, so vision only needs a rough center point.</p><p>Candidates the arm cannot reach (inverse kinematics finds no joint angles that put the gripper there) or whose approach would hit the tote wall (collision checking, which tests the arm's shapes against a model of the cell) are dropped, however well they score. Published learned-grasp results come mostly from household and warehouse objects, so soft, damp food needs its own testing. Without this step, perfect perception and perfect motion can still drop the bunch, because the fingers touch it in the wrong place.</p>",
     "question": {
      "prompt": "<p>The planner's top-scoring grasp needs a wrist tilt that would push a joint past its limit. What should happen?</p>",
      "options": [
       {
        "text": "Drop it and try the next-best candidate, without taking a new picture",
        "correct": true,
        "why": "Planners rank candidates and walk down the list until one passes reach, collision, and placement checks."
       },
       {
        "text": "Run it anyway, since the score already accounts for everything",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "The score rates the contact on the bunch, not whether the arm can get there."
       },
       {
        "text": "Stop the line and call a technician",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "An unreachable grasp is a normal case; the software moves on to another grasp or another bunch."
       },
       {
        "text": "Run inverse kinematics longer until it finds an answer",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Past a joint limit there is no answer to find, and more iterations cannot create one."
       }
      ]
     }
