     "mode": "both",
     "replaces": [],
     "plain": "<p>The packets cross 1,000 km to the operator's office. Like buses on a busy route, they can arrive late, bunched together, or not at all. The distance itself costs only about five thousandths of a second; the plant's cellular link costs far more.</p>",
     "precise": "<p>Propagation (travel time through the cables): light in optical fiber covers about 200 km per millisecond, so 1,000 km costs about 5 ms one way. Real routes are longer than straight lines and add routers and queues. The plant uplink is LTE (a cellular data link, as phones use), which added about 35 ms (median) over a wired network in one teleoperated-driving rig. Area 9's budget uses 48 ms one way for this path (illustrative).</p><p>Two impairments ride along. Jitter (variation in delay from one packet to the next): round trips over LTE from a moving car varied with a 29.5 ms standard deviation in one study. Packet loss (packets that never arrive): a lost video packet damages every frame that refers to it until intra refresh (repairing one stripe of the picture each frame) repairs the picture. Resending helps only if the copy arrives before the frame is due.</p><p>What goes wrong if you judge this stage by a ping (a tiny test packet timed there and back): a ping measures tiny packets, not video frames. On a 5G testbed, sending each frame over the network took about 15 ms of a roughly 193 ms glass-to-glass delay (from light entering the lens to the image leaving the screen).</p>",
     "question": {
      "prompt": "<p>Which single change would shorten this stage the most for this setup?</p>",
      "options": [
       {
        "text": "Move the operator from 1,000 km to 50 km away",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Fiber costs about 5 ms per 1,000 km, so this saves under 5 ms each way. Distance is rarely the big number."
       },
       {
        "text": "Buy ten times more bandwidth on the same LTE link",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "If the link is not congested, capacity is not the problem. LTE's added delay and jitter stay; lag is usually delay and jitter, not capacity."
       },
       {
        "text": "Resend every lost packet until it arrives",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Each resend waits at least a round trip, so the copy arrives after its frame was needed. For live video, a late packet is as useless as a lost one."
       },
       {
        "text": "Replace the plant's LTE uplink with a wired connection",
        "correct": true,
        "why": "LTE added about 35 ms (median) in one rig. In area 9's budget the one-way time drops from 48 to 12 ms, and jitter falls too, which helps the next stop."
       }
      ]
     }
