     "mode": "both",
     "replaces": [],
     "plain": "<p>At the operator's desk the packets wait briefly in a holding line, like a theme park queue that lets riders on at a steady pace even though they arrive in clumps. Then the computer unpacks the picture, and the screen shows it at its next redraw. The holding line is the biggest wait in this part, because the cellular link delivers packets so unevenly.</p>",
     "precise": "<p>Three stages. The jitter buffer (a short holding queue that releases frames on a steady schedule) must cover the spread in arrival times: bigger means fewer stutters but more delay. For this LTE (cellular) link, area 9's budget uses a large 80 ms buffer (illustrative). A wired link has little jitter, and the buffer can shrink to about 15 ms. Decode (unpacking the compressed frame): up to about 10 ms; 8 ms here, and a hardware decoder halves it in the budget (illustrative). Display: the monitor redraws on a fixed beat, so a finished frame waits 0-16.7 ms for the next refresh at 60 Hz (times per second), 8.3 ms on average, plus the monitor's own processing. The budget uses 18 ms in all for the display (illustrative).</p><p>The spread in the range, 98-114 ms, is the refresh wait. The video half of the loop is now done: about 276 ms glass-to-glass (from light entering the camera lens to the matching image leaving the screen) in this budget. Measured rigs did better: 150-200 ms over LTE in one, and about 193 ms over 5G in another.</p><p>What breaks without a jitter buffer: frames that arrive in clumps play in jerks, and a frame that misses its slot is skipped, so the picture must be repaired.</p>",
     "question": {
      "prompt": "<p>At shift change, bulk uploads of inspection images fill the plant's link. What happens to the operator's view?</p>",
      "options": [
       {
        "text": "The view lags tens of ms more, as queues and jitter grow",
        "correct": true,
        "why": "Router queues grow, jitter climbs, and the jitter buffer grows to cover it. Area 9's illustrative case adds 50-80 ms of lag. The fixes are network priority for teleop traffic, a cap on bulk uploads, and an encoder that follows the congestion controller's lower estimate."
       },
       {
        "text": "Nothing, as long as the link has spare capacity on average",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "While the uploads fill the link there is no spare capacity at that moment, whatever the average says. Router buffers fill first, adding delay and jitter for everyone before any packet is dropped."
       },
       {
        "text": "Some frames drop, but the ones that arrive are as fresh as before",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Queues form before drops, so arriving frames are older too, and the jitter buffer holds them longer to smooth them out."
       },
       {
        "text": "A 120 Hz monitor would cancel out the extra lag",
        "correct": false,
        "why": "Going from 60 to 120 Hz cuts the average refresh wait from 8.3 to 4.2 ms, about 4 ms, far less than tens of ms."
       }
      ]
     }
