   "speed": "Max TCP (tool tip) speed 3-5 m/s as listed; lower next to people <sup class=\"cite\">[1, 2, 5]</sup>",
   "notes": "<p>Used where people work nearby or the job moves often, such as machine tending, where the arm is taught by pulling it by hand and moved between machines. Most cobots are six-axis arms that sense contact and cap their speed and force (power and force limiting), which can reduce fencing only if the risk assessment for the whole application allows it, because a blade or sharp part in the gripper can still injure someone <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>. Cobots trade some speed and payload for this, and the listed top speeds are not usable at full value next to people <sup class=\"cite\">[5]</sup>. On the cheese plate line a cobot could fit a shared spot such as a manual rework table (a design judgment), but Universal Robots' g-Series is rated IP65 and ABB's GoFa IP54, not the IP67 the line's spec asks for <sup class=\"cite\">[3, 6]</sup>; the 1,000 mm drawing height is illustrative, not from a datasheet.</p>",
