   "speed": "Travel speed 1.5-2.0 m/s <sup class=\"cite\">[1, 2, 3]</sup>",
   "notes": "<p>Found in warehouses and plants carrying bins, carts, and pallets between fixed points; an AMR (autonomous mobile robot) maps the site and steers around obstacles, while an AGV (automated guided vehicle) follows a fixed route and stops when blocked <sup class=\"cite\">[4]</sup>. It makes material routes reprogrammable without conveyors or forklift drivers, but it parks only to within millimeters (±3 mm at a marker for the MiR250), so an arm mounted on top must re-find its target at every stop <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>. On the cheese plate line AMRs suit moving totes and plate carts around the line (a design judgment), but the MiR250 is rated IP21 and 5-40 °C, so it should stay out of the washdown zone or be parked during cleaning, and it is not rated for the 2-4 °C room <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>. There is no arm reach: the 800 mm shown is the length of the MiR250's 800 x 580 mm footprint, its travel is limited only by the mapped area, and the 300 mm drawing height is illustrative, not from a datasheet <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</p>",
