   "speed": "No pick-cycle figure published; battery runtime 2-4 h <sup class=\"cite\">[1, 3, 4]</sup>",
   "notes": "<p>As of late 2026 mostly in field tests, early deployments, or development; Boston Dynamics describes Atlas re-sorting parts into the order a car line needs them in a field test at a Hyundai facility <sup class=\"cite\">[1, 5]</sup>. The bet is that a human-shaped body can work in places built for people without changing them, but it needs 23-56 independent joint motions, must balance at all times, carries less than many industrial arms, and runs 2-4 hours per battery <sup class=\"cite\">[1, 3, 4]</sup>. Payload runs from about 2-3 kg per arm (Unitree G1) to 30 kg sustained, 50 kg instantaneous (Atlas); reach is 2.2 m (Digit 5, preliminary) to 2.3 m (Atlas); and none of these makers publishes repeatability or a pick-cycle time, so a humanoid cannot yet be compared with an arm on those specs <sup class=\"cite\">[1, 2, 3]</sup>. It has no station on the cheese plate line (a design judgment), since each station is a fixed top-down task in a washdown room and the sources checked show no humanoid rated for food washdown; it is drawn at Atlas's listed 1.9 m height <sup class=\"cite\">[1]</sup>.</p>",
